{"version":"2026.10.11-r25","id":"Q4445841","formula":"GeS","hill":"GeS","elements":["S","Ge"],"composition":{"S":1.0,"Ge":1.0},"names":{"en":"Germanium monosulfide","ru":"Сульфид германия(II)"},"descriptions":{"en":"Germanium monosulfide or Germanium(II) sulfide is the chemical compound with the formula GeS. Germanium sulfide is a red-brown powder or black crystals. It hydrolyzes slowly in moist air but rapidly reacts in water forming Ge(OH)2 and then GeO. It is one of a few sulfides that can be sublimed under vacuum without decomposition. It also forms chalcogenide glasses and is a semiconductor.","ru":"Сульфид германия(II) — бинарное неорганическое соединение металла германия и серы с формулой GeS, серо-чёрные с красным оттенком кристаллы, не растворимые в воде."},"fame":13,"pubchemCid":6367215,"mass":104.7,"charge":0,"smiles":"S=[Ge]","inchikey":"VDNSGQQAZRMTCI-UHFFFAOYSA-N","iupac":"sulfanylidenegermanium","classes":[{"qid":"Q113145171","names":{"en":"type of chemical entity","ru":"тип химического объекта","uk":"тип хімічної сутності"}}],"structureInfo":{"kind":"crystal","source":"materialsproject","ref":"mp-2242","atoms":8,"license":"CC BY 4.0, Materials Project"},"structure":{"cell":[[10.781,0.0,0.0],[0.0,3.603,0.0],[0.0,0.0,4.523]],"kind":"crystal","atoms":[{"x":9.403,"y":0.901,"z":1.659,"el":"Ge"},{"x":1.377,"y":2.702,"z":2.864,"el":"Ge"},{"x":6.768,"y":2.702,"z":3.921,"el":"Ge"},{"x":4.013,"y":0.901,"z":0.602,"el":"Ge"},{"x":7.06,"y":0.901,"z":2.328,"el":"S"},{"x":3.721,"y":2.702,"z":2.195,"el":"S"},{"x":9.112,"y":2.702,"z":0.067,"el":"S"},{"x":1.669,"y":0.901,"z":4.456,"el":"S"}],"bonds":[[0,6,1],[1,7,1],[2,4,1],[3,5,1],[0,4,1],[1,5,1]],"cellOrigin":[0,0,0]},"sources":[{"role":"identity","name":"Wikidata","url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q4445841","license":"CC0 1.0"},{"role":"description","name":"Wikipedia (en)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Germanium_monosulfide","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"},{"role":"description","name":"Wikipedia (ru)","url":"h